abstract: "The first chapter identifies all banking crises in Hong Kong between 1866 and 2010, and investigates their causes and consequences. Based on existing literature, share quotations, and reports from historical newspapers including Hong Kong Daily Press, Hong Kong Telegraph and South China Morning Post, the chapter identifies several banking crises in Hong Kong that have thus far remained undocumented by economic historians. Then it employs a Vector Autoregression (VAR) model as well as data from the Hong Kong Annual Digest of Statistics and the Hong Kong Economic History Database to investigate macroeconomic indicators preceding the crises and economic consequences afterwards. This chapter provides the first long-run chronology of Hong Kong's banking crises, revealing a period of frequent crises from the 1920s to the 1930s. It challenges the conventional focus on banks by highlighting the important role of informal financial institutions, including department stores and grocery stores, in meeting local financial needs."
